<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">College of Cardinals</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The College of Cardinals is the body of senior clergy of the Catholic Church who advise the pope and, since a decree of 1059, elect a new pope in conclave. Its members are appointed by the pope and hold the rank of cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, priest or deacon, many of them heading dioceses or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of the Roman Curia. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> it was dominated by Italian noble families, and popes often appointed their relatives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/college-of-cardinals.jpg" alt="College of Cardinals" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The White House · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardinals_in_red_vestments_during_the_funeral_mass_for_Pope_Francis_in_2025_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
